  "verbalization": "Flavoxate is a cholinergic muscarinic antagonist that is included in the broader class of antimuscarinic agents, which are known to inhibit muscarinic receptors, including the Chrm1 receptor. This relationship is supported by multiple paths in the dataset, all of which indicate that Flavoxate, through its inclusion in the cholinergic muscarinic antagonist class, binds to Chrm1. The lowest common ancestors of Flavoxate and its included compounds, such as Darifenacin, Solifenacin, and Trospium, all fall under the category of organonitrogen heterocyclic compounds and antimuscarinic agents, reinforcing the shared mechanism of action. The consistent evidence across these paths suggests that Flavoxate exerts its effect on Chrm1 through a common pathway, likely involving the inhibition of muscarinic receptors. The mechanism is further supported by the presence of a direct binding interaction between the compounds and Chrm1, as indicated by the paths. The integration of these findings highlights a robust, mechanistically grounded relationship between Flavoxate and Chrm1, consistent with the expected role of antimuscarinic agents in modulating muscarinic receptor activity.",
